From 1437a1bee4390ae0963198323fbd02685e1af2f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Somalapuram Amaranath <somalapuram@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 17:38:08 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] hwc: never report a composition type change for a Client
 layer

SurfaceFlinger rejects every transition out of Client. That is not a preference,
it is the contract -- OutputLayer.cpp:1149-1152 in frameworks/native:

    case Composition::INVALID:
    case Composition::CLIENT:
        result = false;

so a composer may only ever DEMOTE (Device/SolidColor -> Client, or
Cursor/Sideband -> Client|Device). Promotion is always invalid.

GenericCompositionPlanner does not know that. It assigns planes from its own
view of the scene and hands back kDevice/kCursor for layers SurfaceFlinger had
already pinned to Client, and GetChangedCompositionTypes() reported them
verbatim. Observed on an AMD Phoenix laptop, on exactly the layers that make up
the desktop:

    [Wallpaper BBQ wrapper]           Invalid ... Client (1) --> Device (2)
    [VRI-...pclauncher.HomeActivity]  Invalid ... Client (1) --> Device (2)
    [[BBQ] Sprite]                    Invalid ... Client (1) --> Cursor (4)

SurfaceFlinger logs each one and refuses it, so the composer's plan and
SurfaceFlinger's state disagree for that frame.

Clamped here rather than in the planner: the planner's job is to find the best
plan it can, and this is the one place that knows what SurfaceFlinger asked for.

NOT claimed as a fix for the desktop flicker this was found while chasing. The
rejections fire about once a minute, and the reported flicker follows mouse
movement, so the rates do not match; SurfaceFlinger also rejects the change
rather than acting on it. This is a protocol violation worth fixing on its own
merits, and fixing it cleanly removes it as a candidate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01NzM9d6UTbgoE8FxzXbB9QK
Change-Id: I90769bdd37fdb70ccd34614da0347088db87c66d
---
 hwc/HwcDisplay.cpp | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/hwc/HwcDisplay.cpp b/hwc/HwcDisplay.cpp
index 7f3b7ee..5be6e20 100644
--- a/hwc/HwcDisplay.cpp
+++ b/hwc/HwcDisplay.cpp
@@ -324,7 +324,40 @@ auto HwcDisplay::ValidateStagedComposition() -> std::vector<ChangedLayer> {
     ALOGE_IF(it == validated_composition_->composition_types.end(),
              "Backend did not composite layer %" PRId64 "", id);
     if (it != validated_composition_->composition_types.end()) {
-      layer.SetValidatedType(it->second);
+      auto type = it->second;
+
+      // A layer SurfaceFlinger pinned to Client can NEVER be changed, not even
+      // to Device or Cursor. This is not a preference, it is the contract:
+      // OutputLayer.cpp:1149-1152 in frameworks/native has
+      //     case Composition::CLIENT:
+      //         result = false;
+      // so every transition out of Client is rejected, and the composer is only
+      // ever allowed to DEMOTE (Device/SolidColor -> Client, or
+      // Cursor/Sideband -> Client|Device).
+      //
+      // The planner does not know that. GenericCompositionPlanner assigns
+      // planes from its own view of the scene, so on a display where the plan
+      // says a layer could go on hardware it hands back kDevice/kCursor even
+      // when SurfaceFlinger had already decided Client -- and this loop then
+      // reported it verbatim as a change. Observed on an AMD laptop, on exactly
+      // the layers that make up the desktop:
+      //
+      //   [Wallpaper BBQ wrapper]          Client (1) --> Device (2)
+      //   [VRI-...pclauncher.HomeActivity] Client (1) --> Device (2)
+      //   [[BBQ] Sprite]                   Client (1) --> Cursor (4)
+      //
+      // SurfaceFlinger logs each one and refuses it, so hwcomposer's plan and
+      // SurfaceFlinger's state disagree for that frame.
+      //
+      // Clamp here rather than in the planner: the planner's job is to find the
+      // best plan it can, and this is the one place that knows what
+      // SurfaceFlinger actually asked for.
+      if (layer.GetSfType() == CompositionType::kClient &&
+          type != CompositionType::kClient) {
+        type = CompositionType::kClient;
+      }
+
+      layer.SetValidatedType(type);
     }
     if (layer.IsTypeChanged()) {
       changed_layers.emplace_back(id, layer.GetValidatedType());
-- 
